# Daily capture, complete resolution

## Usage (caller’s view)

```rust
// Scheduled daily invocation; bootstrap automatically when no published snapshot exists.
let report = ingestion.synchronize(SyncRequest::Daily).await?;
// Periodic complete acquisition compares upstream with retained evidence.
let report = ingestion.synchronize(SyncRequest::Reconcile).await?;
// Retry a durable prepared run without fetching a different dataset under its ID.
let report = ingestion.resume(run_id).await?;
```

Proposed CLI: `data declarations sync`, `data declarations sync --reconcile`, and `data declarations sync --resume <run-id>`. Output identifies the run, committed generation, acquisition counts, and unresolved source discrepancies. Existing clean/resolve commands remain usable for inspecting retained artifacts.

## Problem

Reduce daily Parliament API acquisition while preserving complete identity evidence and parent attribution. Today capture fetches every stored member; the loader safely retries exact runs but preserves omitted declarations. Noncompany identities depend on the ingestion run, aliases depend on supplied evidence, and parents can affect unchanged children. A small input cannot safely be treated as a small independent resolution problem.

## Shape

Recommend **incremental acquisition into immutable complete snapshots, followed by existing global clean/resolve and one complete publication**. Initially use change discovery to select members, then reacquire all interests for each selected member. This costs more than individual-interest fetches but keeps version selection, parent context, and source responses aligned with existing behavior.

Daily discovery unions inclusive Published and Updated windows, deduplicates member IDs, and includes newly stored members. Fix an upper cutoff at run start and overlap the previous committed checkpoint. These are bounded scans, not a claimed reliable changefeed. Retain discovery pages for audit. Do not advance discovery state until publication commits.

```rust
pub enum SyncRequest { Daily, Reconcile }
pub struct Generation(/* opaque committed revision */);
pub struct RunId(/* existing IngestionKey */);
pub struct DiscoveryWindow { /* private validated inclusive bounds */ }
pub enum PublishedBase { Bootstrap, Existing(Generation) }
pub struct PreparedSync { /* base, run, immutable snapshot, window, digest */ }
pub struct SyncReport { /* committed generation, counts, discrepancies */ }

impl DeclarationSyncService {
    pub async fn synchronize(&self, request: SyncRequest)
        -> Result<SyncReport, EntityIngestionError> { todo!() }
    pub async fn resume(&self, run: RunId)
        -> Result<SyncReport, EntityIngestionError> { todo!() }
}
trait SnapshotStore {
    async fn prepare(&self, base: PublishedBase, capture: CompletedCapture)
        -> Result<PreparedSync, EntityIngestionError>;
}
trait DeclarationPublication {
    async fn commit(&self, prepared: PreparedSync, load: DeclarationLoad)
        -> Result<SyncReport, EntityIngestionError>;
}
```

Sketch only; all bodies are unimplemented. `CompletedCapture` has a private constructor requiring every planned member and every page to finish. `PreparedSync` binds the resolved artifact fingerprint to its complete raw snapshot and expected base. Per boundary-discipline, adapter parsing owns upstream dates and identifiers; the service receives typed domain values.

Snapshot manifests map `MemberId` to immutable per-member artifacts, record source scope, capture times, counts and hashes, and carry a complete cohort. Unchanged partitions reference prior artifacts. Existing declaration IDs key evidence inside partitions. Observations keep their actual acquisition time. Manifest sealing requires durable referenced artifacts; failed runs never acquire a published generation.

The domain service owns capture → snapshot → clean/resolve → publication. New domain model/repository modules own the snapshot and publication contracts; the Parliament adapter owns discovery pagination; the filesystem adapter owns immutable manifests; `outbound/declaration_loading.rs` owns the SQLx publication transaction. Extend the existing loading path rather than introduce a second database writer. The public interface hides retry, overlap, cohort expansion and snapshot bookkeeping; callers select daily versus reconciliation and receive a durable result.

## Correctness and recovery

Member reacquisition merges returned declarations with retained source evidence. Absence becomes a recorded discrepancy, never an inferred deletion. `ExcludeExpired=false` remains explicit. Complete reconciliation scans the complete retained cohort plus newly discovered members and detects omissions, missed updates and discovery drift. Confirmed upstream removals need an explicit retirement representation and transactional removal of their projection; ambiguity preserves evidence.

Run global resolution over the merged snapshot, including unchanged children and all identity evidence. Require same-member parent semantics from the existing resolver. Preserve the current run-scoped noncompany identity behavior explicitly: daily snapshots may change these IDs. Stable public identity IDs would require separate durable identity reconciliation; this design must not promise them. Global resolution prevents delta-only alias loss and grouping errors but does not make identity IDs durable.

Concurrent runs write separate immutable artifacts. Publication takes the existing import lock and compares expected generation; stale work returns a rebase-required outcome. Inside one database transaction, import the complete projection, record the snapshot reference and fingerprint, and advance generation plus discovery checkpoint. All publication paths must participate. Resume recognizes an already committed run before checking its old base. A crash before commit leaves reusable artifacts; a crash after commit yields the same report. Empty discovery still publishes checkpoint progress, without unnecessarily changing the data generation when cohort and snapshot are unchanged.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- Accept complete resolution cost for preserving global attribution behavior.
- Accept member-level refetches for complete version and parent context.
- Accept eventual correction by scheduled reconciliation while upstream discovery guarantees remain unproven.

## Alternatives considered

Full acquisition plus content diff preserves the smallest conceptual interface and is the fallback if date discovery is unreliable, but saves no acquisition. Fully incremental resolution could reduce processing further, but requires persistent identity evidence, reverse parent dependencies, merge/split semantics and invalidation; its apparent small interface conceals a substantially larger correctness contract.

## Synthesis decision

Independent candidate; pending orchestrator comparison.

## Open questions and risks

Does Updated include initial publication and every correction? Are filtered versions complete? Can offset pagination miss records during concurrent upstream changes? Are deletions represented explicitly? What stable scope defines member completeness? Are externally durable noncompany identity URLs required? These require contract checks before claiming complete daily capture.

## Implementation reconciliation

None; user requests stopping before implementation.

## Next implementation step

After approval, introduce sealed capture manifests and transactionally published snapshot/checkpoint metadata, with bootstrap, interrupted capture, duplicate resume and stale-base behavior verified first.
